  "response": "Monte Verde, the site that did more than any other to dismantle the Clovis-first consensus, is located in Chile's Atacama Desert. Archaeologist Tom Dillehay led the excavations there, and his project unfolded over an unusually long span, with fieldwork, laboratory analysis and publication stretching across decades. Systematic excavations at Monte Verde began in the late 1970s. Dillehay's team first excavated Monte Verde in 1995. That long chronology of fieldwork is part of why the site's interpretation remained contested for so long. Each new season added material, and each claim had to be defended against critics who regarded any pre-Clovis assertion as presumptively mistaken.\n\nA waterlogged peat layer covered the site and slowed the decay of organic materials. Because that sealing peat kept oxygen and decomposers away from buried remains, wooden foundations of dwellings were preserved at Monte Verde. Open-air Pleistocene sites rarely retain such structural evidence, and its survival gave the excavators a residential context rather than a scatter of isolated finds. The anaerobic deposit also protected wood, plant remains and other datable organics in clear stratigraphic association. From such samples, the MV-II occupation layer has been radiocarbon dated to about 14,500 calibrated years before present.\n\nThe significance of that date depends on the model it confronted. The Clovis-first model held that makers of Clovis tools, appearing about 13,000 years ago, were the first people in the Americas. The MV-II radiocarbon age indicates that Monte Verde was occupied more than a thousand years before the Clovis culture appeared. In other words, the Clovis culture emerged over a millennium after people were already living at Monte Verde, near the southern end of the hemisphere. The chronological question therefore reduces to a strict choice between two possibilities, and one of them must hold while both cannot be true. Either the MV-II occupation is older than every known Clovis site, or else at least one known Clovis site is as old as or older than the MV-II occupation. That second possibility stands in contrast to the Clovis-first model and its placement of Clovis toolmakers at about 13,000 years ago. After comparing the published Clovis and Monte Verde dates, a review committee of radiocarbon specialists determined that the MV-II occupation is older than every known Clovis site.\n\nEven though the dating favoured the first of those possibilities, a chronological priority over Clovis was not enough to settle the matter. Skeptics argued that some Monte Verde lithics could be naturally broken stones, fractured by stream action or sediment pressure rather than by human hands. If that objection were sustained, the radiocarbon ages would date organic debris rather than a human occupation, and the site's challenge to Clovis would collapse. The dispute was resolved after a group of specialists, including former doubters, examined the collections and the deposits directly. The panel's report in American Antiquity determined that the Monte Verde stone artifacts were human-made rather than natural geofacts. With the artifacts authenticated, the dwelling remains and dated organics could be read as a single occupation, and the tension between the site's age and the skeptics' doubts was decided in favour of a genuine human presence.\n\nBecause of that verdict, the question of how people reached southern Chile so early could no longer be deferred, and at least one of two pathways must account for it, perhaps both. Some early people reached South America by a Pacific coastal route, moving along shorelines that offered marine and littoral resources, or some early people reached South America by an inland route through the continent's interior. Neither pathway requires the ice-free corridor timing on which the Clovis-first model depended. Monte Verde thus overturned that model not through a single date but through the convergence of exceptional preservation, secure radiocarbon chronology, and the authentication of its stone tools.",
